## 🗣️ How to Present This to the Panel

> [!IMPORTANT]
> The key shift is from **"we used ML to predict delays"** (which any student can do) to **"we solved specific technical challenges in applying ML to Sri Lankan construction delay prediction"**

Frame your presentation around these **technical challenge narratives**:

1. **"Uncertainty quantification in small-sample construction data"** — Monte Carlo Dropout or quantile regression to provide reliable confidence intervals, not just point predictions

2. **"Multi-model explainability for non-technical stakeholders"** — Using SHAP to translate black-box predictions into actionable insights that construction managers can understand and trust

3. **"Domain-adapted ensemble learning"** — Why a single model isn't enough for heterogeneous construction projects across 25 districts with varying climate, infrastructure, and contractor ecosystems

4. **"Real-time sensitivity analysis"** — Computing and visualizing feature contributions on-the-fly to enable project managers to do scenario planning before the project starts

These are all **genuine research-worthy challenges** that will completely change the panel's perception from "used a library" to "solved a hard problem."
